A||||6||Richard Parnell||Thermosta Revisited||||9
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||11-08-1999||02:08 PM||||Sorry to bring this up again, but.  I replaced my thermostat on my 87 sc 5 cyl.  It was running cool (below the first line) continually and I figured this was not helping my fuel economy.  So I put in a new thermostat from NAPA.  Now it does the strangest thing.  It slowly goes from the first mark up to 220 or even up to right below the red line and the goes back down to the first line.  It will do this the entire time I am driving even if I'm accelerating up a hill or sitting in traffic.  Back and forth like a slow pendulum.. Should I get rid of the new thermostat, is it bad. Help!||||reg||9
Z||000001||ChadMan||11-08-1999||03:19 PM||||Hate to say it, but it sounds like your new thermostat is opening too late.  I got one from a Nationwise (remember them?) for my old 84 once that was bad right out of the box.  Neither my 84 nor my 88 4 bangers ever approach the red zone - not even idling on a 90-degree day.  <P>Try removing your thermostat and putting it in a pot of water on the stove.  Then heat up the water.  The thermostat should open fully before the water boils.  When you turn off the heat, the water should quit boiling before the thermostat closes.  If it doesn't, you've got a bad 'stat.||||reg||13
Z||000002||88 Fiero||11-08-1999||03:28 PM||||Chad is right ....you'd probably want to check that thermostat first...looks like it might be a defect one...||||reg||1
Z||000003||sinoloa||11-08-1999||11:21 PM||||check your thermostat sensor.  It's odd that a new thermostat would go bad so fast.||||reg||3
Z||000004||theogre||11-09-1999||01:41 AM||||What the #@!! year did they put a 5 cyl in these cars?<P>Let's assume it's a 4 cyl,,,,, If you still have the old thermostat, look on it and see what temp it is. There is usually a number on the bulb.<P>If you mean the first un-numbered line, at about 1/3 the gauge, then the old one was likely a 185. 4 cyl motors run just fine with 185 thermostats in them. Put in another one. ((A 185 will not hurt your milage. I put 22K miles on mine in the last year. I get 30+ highway. 25+ mixed. and that's with a 185 Stant thermostat. Stant is available anywhere. Likely your NAPA one is a Stant.))<P>Sounds like this one is bad if the old one was stable and this one aint.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-09-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Richard Parnell||11-09-1999||03:37 PM||||Thanks everyone and theogre, it is a 4 cyl. "5" spd. Just didn't proof read.  I was wondering if I had an air bubble or something in the water line.  I'm changing back to the above recommended low temp thermo. Thanks again guys.||||reg||14
Z||000006||theogre||11-09-1999||07:41 PM||||if the thermostat neck is filled to the top, with the stat out, it's not an air pocket. at least not in the block.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-09-1999).]||||reg||1
